{"assets":["/editorial-assets/50844ebf114b4c2a3a97069c6247c7880b154d45d236f11d2bf65cdeb12582f8.png","/editorial-assets/c0f275991a040050d6d9fc312cfbf778648d291b063e0d855de4c1448aae4537.png","/editorial-assets/099c6d9ff606dd3f587e501ec03a15eeece2701d1b6755df811257a35484d322.png","/editorial-assets/ce354180167eb78ce5698be7142f8d2609c27c3d1a53895160c25c8848da9b6a.png","/editorial-assets/e86804b73042d040fc63d809f0d5501c9e5a7240133ae81f081cb4e174da7a89.png","/editorial-assets/e5cf35d405d7aaa6fe3a6f092c72132575642a012e9be0de14e0084f2a808677.png","/editorial-assets/d7f587116aa9a9abae16c1281b3f7d4881999b5a5ed78d30ebb626b7c24209d8.png","/editorial-assets/191e85fb2fdba28874df2b23731791627bbcbbc7bc6b57db78315d32355273ba.png","/editorial-assets/3f4691392a95c241f0e8ab63a860880c472613b2bdc07a8a41b17f66502042d6.png","/editorial-assets/6e557a0cc9116ac5d19f75b2698c80c27e3f5acd6033eef0e37fda92916d8f74.png","/editorial-assets/95c696dd2c076b1c8566089a8316e4cc289909f9bcd37c0c6d8fb83622213f9e.png","/editorial-assets/2a0c55a2eca19d828c19abe132294f0a73dcb82a30bbc44e237ee84d281a0711.png","/editorial-assets/630a60819a2339658b6eb63426a98d370ea635e8f69ad1071b413ad48b7cc604.png","/editorial-assets/879096affedeedfa5e2d3c9ee27d1c6fe05096d759d6f27e426c3d9528f7ea28.png","/editorial-assets/f8c560ff37e8152c71b6ed0c7bcd13efb3d7b01ec2c26e5b41815f685032f483.png","/editorial-assets/3182970bf403163862cb448e9b43757b4c8b62d3807aef8e87647e50746294df.png"],"contestId":"760","diagnostics":[],"document":{"children":[{"attrs":{"problemCode":"760A"},"children":[{"attrs":{"level":3},"children":[{"attrs":{"href":"https://codeforces.com/contest/760/problem/A"},"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"760A - Petr and a calendar"}],"kind":"link"}],"kind":"heading"},{"children":[{"children":[{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"Just implement writing dates one by one and keeping current column and row, or use the formula "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"answer"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" = (("},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"d"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" - 1) + "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"ndays"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" - 1) / 7 + 1, where "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"ndays"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" is the number of days in the month."}],"kind":"paragraph"}],"kind":"container"}],"kind":"container"}],"kind":"problem_section"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"Problem author: "},{"attrs":{"href":"https://codeforces.com/profile/KAN"},"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"KAN"}],"kind":"link"},{"kind":"text","text":"."}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"attrs":{"problemCode":"760B"},"children":[{"attrs":{"level":3},"children":[{"attrs":{"href":"https://codeforces.com/contest/760/problem/B"},"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"760B - Frodo and pillows"}],"kind":"link"}],"kind":"heading"},{"children":[{"children":[{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"Let's do binary search on the answer. How to check if Frodo can have "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"x"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" pillows or more? We need to calculate the least amount of pillows we need to give to all the hobbits and compare it to m."}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"The number of pillows is minimized if we give "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"x"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" - 1 pillows to Frodo's neighbors, "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"x"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" - 2 pillows to the hobbits at the distance 2 from Frodo and so on, until we reach 1 pillow or until we reach an end of beds row. The total amount of pillows on one side of Frodo can be calculated using a formula."}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"Suppose there are "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"y"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" beds on one side of Frodo. There are two cases: if "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"y"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" > "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"x"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" - 1, then the total number of pillows is "},{"attrs":{"alt":"","src":"/editorial-assets/50844ebf114b4c2a3a97069c6247c7880b154d45d236f11d2bf65cdeb12582f8.png"},"kind":"image"},{"kind":"text","text":", otherwise the total number of pillows is "},{"attrs":{"alt":"","src":"/editorial-assets/c0f275991a040050d6d9fc312cfbf778648d291b063e0d855de4c1448aae4537.png"},"kind":"image"},{"kind":"text","text":"."}],"kind":"paragraph"}],"kind":"container"}],"kind":"container"}],"kind":"problem_section"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"Problem author: "},{"attrs":{"href":"https://codeforces.com/profile/KAN"},"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"KAN"}],"kind":"link"},{"kind":"text","text":"."}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"attrs":{"problemCode":"756A"},"children":[{"attrs":{"level":3},"children":[{"attrs":{"href":"https://codeforces.com/contest/756/problem/A"},"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"756A - Pavel and barbecue"}],"kind":"link"}],"kind":"heading"},{"children":[{"children":[{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"At first, let's deal with the permutation. We can see that "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"p"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" should have exactly one cycle to suit Pavel. The minimum number of changes is 0 if there is only one cycle, and the number of cycles if there is more than one cycle."}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"What should we do with "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"b"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":"? We can see that the skewers visit a particular position "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"x"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" in the same direction again and again if and only if the total number of ones in "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"b"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" is even. If the total number of ones in "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"b"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" is odd, then each time a skewer visits a particular position "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"x"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":", it has direction different from the previous time. Thus, the condition is satisfied if and only if the number of ones in "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"b"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" is odd. We should add 1 to the answer if it isn't."}],"kind":"paragraph"}],"kind":"container"}],"kind":"container"}],"kind":"problem_section"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"Problem author: "},{"attrs":{"href":"https://codeforces.com/profile/pashka"},"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"pashka"}],"kind":"link"},{"kind":"text","text":"."}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"attrs":{"problemCode":"756B"},"children":[{"attrs":{"level":3},"children":[{"attrs":{"href":"https://codeforces.com/contest/756/problem/B"},"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"756B - Travel Card"}],"kind":"link"}],"kind":"heading"},{"children":[{"children":[{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"Hint: the problem looks difficult because tickets can change, however, it's can be solved with simple dynamic programming. You are asked the difference between neighboring dp's subtasks."}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"More detailed editorial will be added soon."}],"kind":"paragraph"}],"kind":"container"}],"kind":"container"}],"kind":"problem_section"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"Problem author: "},{"attrs":{"href":"https://codeforces.com/profile/pashka"},"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"pashka"}],"kind":"link"},{"kind":"text","text":"."}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"attrs":{"problemCode":"756C"},"children":[{"attrs":{"level":3},"children":[{"attrs":{"href":"https://codeforces.com/contest/756/problem/C"},"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"756C - Nikita and stack"}],"kind":"link"}],"kind":"heading"},{"children":[{"children":[{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"Hint 1: look at the operations in the reverse order. Let's count the balance for each prefix, i.e. the difference between the number of push(x) operations and the number of pop() operations."}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"Hint 2: Now we have to find the first operation that makes balance positive. This can be done using segment tree."}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"Solution: Let's reverse the order of operations. Now we can see that on the top of the stack will be the first integer added with push(x) such that the number of pop() operations and the number of push(x) operations before this operation is equal, if there is one. Let's keep for each position a value called balance: the number of push(x) operations minus the number of pop() operations before and including this position. To find the answer, we should find the first position with posivive balance. When we add an operation, we should add -1 or 1 to all posisions starting with the position of the operation, depending on the type of the operation."}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"To cope with the operations quickly, we can store the balance in a segment tree. The addition is done with lazy propogation, finding the first position with positive balance can be done in two ways. First way is to perform binary search on the answer and then query the segment tree for maximim on some prefix. The compexity is "},{"attrs":{"alt":"","src":"/editorial-assets/099c6d9ff606dd3f587e501ec03a15eeece2701d1b6755df811257a35484d322.png"},"kind":"image"},{"kind":"text","text":" per query then. The other way is to walk down the tree always moving to the leftmost son with positive maximum. When we reach the leaf, the position of this leaf is the answer. The complexity is "},{"attrs":{"alt":"","src":"/editorial-assets/ce354180167eb78ce5698be7142f8d2609c27c3d1a53895160c25c8848da9b6a.png"},"kind":"image"},{"kind":"text","text":" per query."}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"The overall complexity is "},{"attrs":{"alt":"","src":"/editorial-assets/e86804b73042d040fc63d809f0d5501c9e5a7240133ae81f081cb4e174da7a89.png"},"kind":"image"},{"kind":"text","text":" or "},{"attrs":{"alt":"","src":"/editorial-assets/e5cf35d405d7aaa6fe3a6f092c72132575642a012e9be0de14e0084f2a808677.png"},"kind":"image"},{"kind":"text","text":" depending on the realization."}],"kind":"paragraph"}],"kind":"container"}],"kind":"container"}],"kind":"problem_section"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"Problem author: "},{"attrs":{"href":"https://codeforces.com/profile/sivukhin"},"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"sivukhin"}],"kind":"link"},{"kind":"text","text":"."}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"attrs":{"problemCode":"756D"},"children":[{"attrs":{"level":3},"children":[{"attrs":{"href":"https://codeforces.com/contest/756/problem/D"},"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"756D - Bacterial Melee"}],"kind":"link"}],"kind":"heading"},{"children":[{"children":[{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"Hint: find a condition when a string is reachable from another string in terms of subsequences, then apply DP for counting suitable subsequences."}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"Solution: How to determine if a string can be obtained from another string after a number of operations? It helps to consider (maximal) blocks of adjacent characters. Let us define "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"comp"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":"("},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"s"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":") as a \"compressed\" version of "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"s"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" that keeps a single character from each block of "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"s"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" in the same order, for example, "},{"attrs":{"alt":"","src":"/editorial-assets/d7f587116aa9a9abae16c1281b3f7d4881999b5a5ed78d30ebb626b7c24209d8.png"},"kind":"image"},{"kind":"text","text":". It is not hard to see that each operation either preserves the sequence of blocks (if we ignore their lengths) or erases a single block (when it consists of a single character and is overwritten by an adjacent character). This is the same as saying that each operation applied to "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"s"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" either doesn't change "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"comp"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":"("},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"s"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":") or erases a single character from it. Therefore, if "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"t"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" can be obtained from "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"s"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" after a number of operations, then "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"comp"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":"("},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"t"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":") must be a subsequence of "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"comp"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":"("},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"s"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":")."}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"In fact, this is an \"if and only if\" condition. Indeed, suppose that "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"comp"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":"("},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"t"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":") is a subsequence of "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"comp"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":"("},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"s"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":"). We start our process of making "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"t"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" from "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"s"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" by eliminating the blocks not present in "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"t"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":", effectively making "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"comp"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":"("},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"s"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":") = "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"comp"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":"("},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"t"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":"). After that, it can be seen that the borders of the blocks can be moved rather freely (but carefully enough not to murder any block) so that "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"s"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" can be indeed made equal to "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"t"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":"."}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"We can see from the last argument that from the reachability point of view the two strings "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"s"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" and "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"t"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" are essentially different if "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"comp"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":"("},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"s"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":") ≠ "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"comp"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":"("},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"t"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":"). If "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"s"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" is the given string, and a string "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"u"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" is a subsequence of "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"comp"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":"("},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"s"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":") that doesn't have equal adjacent characters, then there are "},{"attrs":{"alt":"","src":"/editorial-assets/191e85fb2fdba28874df2b23731791627bbcbbc7bc6b57db78315d32355273ba.png"},"kind":"image"},{"kind":"text","text":" reachable strings with "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"comp"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":"(...) = "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"u"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":"; indeed, this is exactly the number of ways to split "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"n"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" characters into |"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"u"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":"| non-empty blocks."}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"Now, for each "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"l"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" we have to count the number of subsequences of "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"s"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" with length "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"l"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" that do not have equal adjacent characters. There are several ways to do that. One way is storing "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"dp"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"children":[{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"c"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":", "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"l"}],"kind":"emphasis"}],"kind":"subscript"},{"kind":"text","text":" — the number of valid subsequences of length "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"l"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" that end in character "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"c"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":". We now process characters one by one and recompute values of "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"dp"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"children":[{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"c"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":", "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"l"}],"kind":"emphasis"}],"kind":"subscript"},{"kind":"text","text":". If the new character is "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"c"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"children":[{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"i"}],"kind":"emphasis"}],"kind":"subscript"},{"kind":"text","text":", then clearly values of "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"dp"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"children":[{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"c"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":", "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"l"}],"kind":"emphasis"}],"kind":"subscript"},{"kind":"text","text":" with "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"c"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" ≠ "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"c"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"children":[{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"i"}],"kind":"emphasis"}],"kind":"subscript"},{"kind":"text","text":" do not change. We can also see that we can find the new values of "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"dp"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"children":[{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"c"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"children":[{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"i"}],"kind":"emphasis"}],"kind":"subscript"},{"kind":"text","text":", "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"l"}],"kind":"emphasis"}],"kind":"subscript"},{"kind":"text","text":" with the formula "}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"attrs":{"alt":"","src":"/editorial-assets/3f4691392a95c241f0e8ab63a860880c472613b2bdc07a8a41b17f66502042d6.png"},"kind":"image"},{"kind":"text","text":" Indeed, consider a valid subsequence that ends in "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"c"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"children":[{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"i"}],"kind":"emphasis"}],"kind":"subscript"},{"kind":"text","text":". If we take any occurence of this subsequence, we are free to move the last character to the newly appended occurence of "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"c"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"children":[{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"i"}],"kind":"emphasis"}],"kind":"subscript"},{"kind":"text","text":". It follows that there is a bijection between valid subsequences that end in "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"c"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"children":[{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"i"}],"kind":"emphasis"}],"kind":"subscript"},{"kind":"text","text":" and valid subsequences that end in anything but "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"c"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"children":[{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"i"}],"kind":"emphasis"}],"kind":"subscript"},{"kind":"text","text":" (that includes an empty subsequence). This solution takes "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"O"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":"("},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"kn"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"2"}],"kind":"superscript"},{"kind":"text","text":") time (where "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"k"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" = 26 is the number of different characters), but can easily by optimized to "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"O"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":"("},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"n"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"2"}],"kind":"superscript"},{"kind":"text","text":") time."}],"kind":"container"}],"kind":"container"}],"kind":"problem_section"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"Problem author: "},{"attrs":{"href":"https://codeforces.com/profile/Endagorion"},"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"Endagorion"}],"kind":"link"},{"kind":"text","text":"."}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"attrs":{"problemCode":"756E"},"children":[{"attrs":{"level":3},"children":[{"attrs":{"href":"https://codeforces.com/contest/756/problem/E"},"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"756E - Byteland coins"}],"kind":"link"}],"kind":"heading"},{"children":[{"children":[{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"Let's calculate DP[pref][x] — number of ways to pay "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"x"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" tugriks using only "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"pref"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" first types. Of course, "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"x"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" can be very big, but we will store DP only for those "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"x"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" which are not bigger than the sum of all the coins of first "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"pref"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" types and can lead to answer: "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"x"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" = "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"k"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":"·"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"D"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" + ("},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"m"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":"%"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"D"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":") where "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"D"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" is the last coin denomination."}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"Every next layer of this DP can be calculated in "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"O"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":"("},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"sz"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"children":[{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"i"}],"kind":"emphasis"}],"kind":"subscript"},{"kind":"text","text":") time using prefix sums where "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"sz"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" is the size of the new layer. "},{"attrs":{"alt":"","src":"/editorial-assets/6e557a0cc9116ac5d19f75b2698c80c27e3f5acd6033eef0e37fda92916d8f74.png"},"kind":"image"},{"kind":"text","text":". "}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"attrs":{"alt":"","src":"/editorial-assets/95c696dd2c076b1c8566089a8316e4cc289909f9bcd37c0c6d8fb83622213f9e.png"},"kind":"image"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"Now all that remains is to calculate some info about "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"m"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":"%"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"D"}],"ki